                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ce9055d2-c8eb-359d-912c-4c4c29a48f2c",
                        "entry_id": "c055f3c8-4399-3b06-8b50-80e6e9f3728c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Sorte de phénakistiscope inventé par William George Horner en 1833, montrant les différentes phases du mouvement chez les êtres animés."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a176ed6d-751f-4e61-9f87-8a1ea72def4a",
                                "sense_id": "ce9055d2-c8eb-359d-912c-4c4c29a48f2c",
                                "text": "[…] en outre, différents auteurs adaptèrent à des zootropes, soit des figures construites d’après mes notations chronographiques, soit des images obtenues par le célèbre photographe américain, et obtinrent ainsi une représentation saisissante d’animaux en mouvement."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1234ecfa-dc96-4c3a-98e9-186ae9318e00",
                                "sense_id": "ce9055d2-c8eb-359d-912c-4c4c29a48f2c",
                                "text": "Un si modeste remue-ménage ne vaudrait pas d’être mentionné, s’il ne servait à faire comprendre que je n’eus, pendant quelque temps, que de rapides images de Marco, qui se succèdent saccadées comme des vues du zootrope."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "884f0ab1-a177-4e7f-a0a4-b9d6f5237136",
                        "source_entry_id": "c055f3c8-4399-3b06-8b50-80e6e9f3728c",
                        "source_sense_id": "ce9055d2-c8eb-359d-912c-4c4c29a48f2c",
                        "target_lang_word": "zoetrope",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "zoetrope",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2fc953b8-12c6-309f-be6c-386d686b93e3",
                                        "entry_id": "bc2300a7-9a38-3cfc-8c86-252c75572a0f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An optical toy, in which figures made to revolve on the inside of a cylinder, and viewed through slits in its circumference, appear like a single figure passing through a series of natural motions as if animated or mechanically moved."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8fc7f56f-f441-4e71-bfb1-b2711eb68c5f",
                                                "sense_id": "2fc953b8-12c6-309f-be6c-386d686b93e3",
                                                "text": "Is consciousness really discontinuous, incessantly interrupted and recommencing (from the psychologist’s point of view)? and does it only seem continuous to itself by an illusion analogous to that of the zoetrope?"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ede30a8f-d6ba-45fa-9ba9-ec887b870d5a",
                                                "sense_id": "2fc953b8-12c6-309f-be6c-386d686b93e3",
                                                "text": "This was the way I passed through the remainder of my childhood. The zoetrope span smoothly, time’s Chief Designer narrowed the legs of trousers and decreed that the cars should be more aerodynamic."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "22133e2b-a30d-4990-a65d-20b82715464c",
                                                "sense_id": "2fc953b8-12c6-309f-be6c-386d686b93e3",
                                                "text": "The Rosenbloom Loop is a clever little device, but it’s an even more clever symbol of the role that discipline plays in the creation of illusion: the persistence of vision that makes sequential still images appear to move. In a sense, that’s the wizardry that Grand spins in this zoetrope of a novel as these characters love and build and pine and die."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
